<h2 id="readino-us-launch">FBReader's Readino Ebook Store Arrives in the US</h2>

<p>The FBReader team has launched <strong>Readino</strong>, an independent ebook store, in the United States after a successful debut in the United Kingdom five months ago and a subsequent expansion to Australia. Readino offers over <strong>1.5 million English-language titles</strong> from mainstream UK and US publishers, distributed through a major British ebook distributor.</p>

<p>FBReader itself is no newcomer to the ebook space. First released in 2005, it is a highly customizable, lightweight cross-platform ebook reader with over <strong>30 million installations</strong> worldwide. Its proprietary rendering engine is known for speed and broad format support — EPUB, EPUB3, MOBI, AZW3, FB2, CBR, CBZ, plain text, HTML, RTF, and Microsoft Word (.doc) files all work out of the box.</p>

<p>What makes Readino different from the Kindle Store, Google Play Books, or Apple Books is its <strong>"open" philosophy</strong>. Every ebook sold on Readino uses standard, non-exclusive Readium LCP encryption. This means you buy a book as an EPUB file that you own — not a license locked to a single app or device ecosystem. You can download your EPUB and read it in FBReader, transfer it to a Kobo, sideload it onto an Android e-reader, or store it in your Calibre library. The storefront itself is accessible on Android, Windows, macOS, and Linux.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-readino">What Is Readino?</h2>

<p>Readino is an independent ebook shop created by the FBReader team. Unlike the major platform stores, Readino does not require a proprietary device or a specific app ecosystem. Its business model is simple: sell EPUB files with Readium LCP encryption, let customers download and read them however they choose.</p>

